- Bugbot or the agentic security review commented → skeptical posture. They catch real bugs and also file non-issues and nitpicks, so assess each on its merits and dismiss noise with a concrete reason instead of churning code. Triage fix / dismiss / ask per `references/bugbot-triage.md`.
- Broken skill mid-task → fix it in its own PR. Don't block. Don't silently work around it.
- Long, autonomous, or multi-phase work, or any task the user steps away from to review later ("going to bed", "trust it when i'm back", "keep going until X") → a decision trail via the **show-me-your-work** skill. Commit it when stakes need an auditable record. Keep it local otherwise.

=== entry 52f833ea1a0e
kind: translation
rules: agent-brief, invoke
intent: Playbook-step subagents are briefed to read the poteto-agent prompt file (resolved to an absolute path) and given this skill's path, since T3 has no agent type field. Preserves that the receiving chat runs the playbook itself and that routed skills keep their own briefs and models. The chat that runs the playbook is named by the skill the user started, with no slash.
--- upstream
**Use `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"` for any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). The chat that receives `/poteto-mode` runs the playbook. Never spawn a `poteto-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type` for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `poteto-agent`.
--- patched
**Brief any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step as a `poteto-agent`** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). Resolve `../pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` (relative to this skill's folder) and this file to absolute paths, then start the brief "Read `<abs>/pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` and act as that agent. The poteto-mode skill is at `<abs>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`." The chat where the user starts the **poteto-mode** skill runs the playbook. Never spawn a `poteto-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own agent briefs for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `poteto-agent`.

=== entry 760047fd626b
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, read-only, capabilities, model-role, model-unnamed, invoke
intent: Spawn defaults in T3 terms: async `delegate_task` with the whole brief and its own request ID, no `runtimeMode`, and models from the project models file or willyskills's role defaults. Preserves the difficulty tiering (hardest changes to `hardest tasks`, trivial edits to `feature, refactoring`) and that per-role lines override the routed skills' choices. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash.
--- upstream
**Defaults for every `Task` call.** `run_in_background: true`, agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-pstack`. Defaults `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` for code, `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (`claude-opus-5-5-xhigh`), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model. Per-role lines in the `/setup-pstack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Task `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
--- patched
**Defaults for every `delegate_task` call.** `mode: "async"`, the inherited runtime mode (never set `runtimeMode`, a narrower mode makes the child wait for approvals no one can give), the whole brief in `task` with file pointers not inlined context, its own `clientRequestId` (reused only to retry that spawn), explicit model per role (configurable with the **setup-pstack** skill. Defaults {{model:feature, refactoring}} for code, {{model:judgment and prose}} for prose and judgment). Call `orchestrator_capabilities` once before the first spawn. Keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. The child's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling, and use `task_status` only for a result needed mid-turn. Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (the `hardest tasks` role, {{model:hardest tasks}} by default), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model (the `feature, refactoring` role). Per-role lines in {{snippet:models-file}} override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). The **setup-pstack** skill writes that file. A role with no line keeps its default. {{snippet:model-entry}} Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.

=== entry 74fb1b31c08c
kind: translation
rules: model-effort, model-role, invoke
intent: The role's effort is a ceiling: lower it for judgment and routine work, never raise it, and never use fast mode. Preserves that verification stays on the `swarm workers` model and that only effort changes, so a diverse panel stays diverse; the rejected-slug fallback is dropped. The setup-pstack skill is named with no slash.
--- upstream
**Match the model to the job.** Before each spawn, start from the role's line or default and pick the reasoning level and speed the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting level, xhigh by default. Raise it one step, up to max, for the hardest or subtlest work. Lower it one step for judgment and two or more steps for routine work. Use fast mode only when the job is urgent, meaning CI or trunk is red or a person is blocked on the result right now. Otherwise use standard speed, the same slug without `-fast`. Verification is the exception and stays on the `swarm workers` model and speed (`grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` by default). Change only the effort level and whether the slug is fast, and keep the family the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. If the family has no slug at that level and speed, use its nearest slug. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Task tool rejects the adjusted slug, do not try another adjusted slug. Use the role's line or default and say so.
--- patched
**Match the model to the job.** Before each spawn, start from the role's line or default and pick the reasoning level the job needs. The **setup-pstack** skill's budget sets each role's starting level, and that level is a ceiling. Keep it for the hardest or subtlest work. Lower it one step for judgment and two or more steps for routine work. Never raise it, and never set `fastMode`. Verification is the exception and stays on the `swarm workers` model and effort ({{model:swarm workers}} by default). Change only the effort level, and keep the model the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. If the model has no effort option at that level, use its nearest level below. `orchestrator_capabilities` lists each model's options. A role on `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model.

=== entry 3863f36924b9
kind: translation
rules: todolist
intent: The visible step list is `update_plan` in Codex and a list kept in the reply in Claude Code. Preserves that the playbook's steps come first, verbatim, with skipped steps kept and given a reason.
--- upstream
Open a todolist whose first items are the matched playbook's steps, copied in verbatim, before any task-specific todos. A step you choose not to do stays in the list with a one-line `skip: <reason>`. Match the task to a playbook below and open its full steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case, hyphens kept and other punctuation dropped, unless the entry gives its file. Copy its steps in verbatim.
--- patched
Open a step list whose first items are the matched playbook's steps, copied in verbatim, before any task-specific todos. Codex keeps it with `update_plan`. Claude Code keeps it in the reply and updates it at each phase. A step you choose not to do stays in the list with a one-line `skip: <reason>`. Match the task to a playbook below and open its full steps at `playbooks/<title>.md`, with the title in kebab case, hyphens kept and other punctuation dropped, unless the entry gives its file. Copy its steps in verbatim.

=== entry 1bec9809dcd4
kind: translation
rules: cursor-only, loop
intent: Drops the cloud-agent URL from Session pickup's sources and the Cursor restart from Pause safely's triggers, and rewrites the quoted user phrase "/loop until X" as "keep going until X". Every playbook name and description otherwise stays verbatim, since routing matches on them.
--- upstream
- **Investigation.** Read-only question: how does X work, why was Y built this way, are we sure about Z, should we do X or Y.
- **Bug fix.** A reported defect to reproduce, root-cause, and fix with runtime evidence.
- **Perf issue.** A measured slowness to trace and improve against a baseline.
- **Hillclimb.** Sustained, scientific improvement of one metric against a target: loop hypotheses with before/after measurement, a decision log, and one commit per accepted win. Distinct from Perf issue, which is a one-off fix.
- **Runtime forensics.** Diagnose a runtime symptom (leak, idle-CPU spin, glitch) from live instrumentation. The deliverable is a diagnosis, not a fix.
- **Trace forensics.** Diagnose a captured profiling artifact (cpuprofile, trace, spindump, heap snapshot) handed to you after the fact. The deliverable is a diagnosis, not a fix.
- **Feature.** New or changed behavior, built from a named data shape.
- **Refactoring.** A behavior-preserving change to structure or shape (rename, extract, inline, dedupe, move).
- **Prototype.** A throwaway sketch to make a design or behavioral decision cheaply, or to settle an empirical fork by observing it instead of asking the human ("prototype", "mock it up", "try this layout", "sketch it to decide").
- **Visual parity.** Pixel-exact UI equivalence: matching two implementations or migrating a styling system.
- **Authoring or modifying a skill.** Writing or editing a SKILL.md. File: `playbooks/authoring-a-skill.md`.
- **Eval.** Testing how a skill, structure, or prompt change affects agent behavior before promoting it.
- **Babysit.** Driving a PR or a stack to merge-ready: conflicts, review threads, CI.
- **Shipping.** The half after Babysit. Independently verifying a green stack, then landing the contiguous verified run bottom-up through `gh` by default or Origin when its CLI is available.
- **Autonomous run.** A long task to drive to completion without stopping ("run until done", "/loop until X").
- **Orchestrate.** A standing project handed to one coordinator chat: multi-day, many stacked PRs, dozens to hundreds of subagents, minimal human turns ("run this whole project", "own this migration until it lands"). Distinct from Autonomous run, which drives one task to a predicate. Work one agent could finish inside the session's budget routes there, not here, however program-shaped the phrasing sounds.
- **Autopilot-full.** A queue of independent PRs run to merged with full autonomy. One owner per PR carries build through merge, and the root swarm-verifies each PR before its owner merges ("autopilot this queue", "full autopilot", one-owner-per-PR programs).
- **Autopilot-stack.** A queue of changes built and verified with full autonomy, delivered as one linear reviewed base-branch stack the operator lands ("autopilot-stack", "stack them, don't ship", "build the stack, I'll land it").
- **Session pickup.** Resuming or taking over a prior agent's in-flight work from a transcript, cloud-agent URL, or pushed branch.
- **Pause safely.** Suspending in-flight work cleanly so it can be resumed, on an explicit pause, going offline, a Cursor restart, or imminent context compaction. The complement to Session pickup.
- **Multi-phase or multi-PR plan.** Work that spans phases or stacked PRs. File: `playbooks/multi-phase-plan.md`.
- **Worktree and simulator cleanup.** Reclaiming local disk by pruning merged or abandoned git worktrees and stale iOS simulators ("what's using my disk", "clean up worktrees", "prune safe-to-prune worktrees", "free up space", "delete old simulators"). File: `playbooks/worktree-cleanup.md`.
- **Opening a PR.** Invoked at the end of every other playbook.
--- patched
- **Investigation.** Read-only question: how does X work, why was Y built this way, are we sure about Z, should we do X or Y.
- **Bug fix.** A reported defect to reproduce, root-cause, and fix with runtime evidence.
- **Perf issue.** A measured slowness to trace and improve against a baseline.
- **Hillclimb.** Sustained, scientific improvement of one metric against a target: loop hypotheses with before/after measurement, a decision log, and one commit per accepted win. Distinct from Perf issue, which is a one-off fix.
- **Runtime forensics.** Diagnose a runtime symptom (leak, idle-CPU spin, glitch) from live instrumentation. The deliverable is a diagnosis, not a fix.
- **Trace forensics.** Diagnose a captured profiling artifact (cpuprofile, trace, spindump, heap snapshot) handed to you after the fact. The deliverable is a diagnosis, not a fix.
- **Feature.** New or changed behavior, built from a named data shape.
